/table>
<p><b>Apollo 11</b> (July 16=E2=80=9324, 1969) was the American <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spaceflight" title=3D"Spaceflight">spacefligh=
t</a> that first landed <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human" tit=
le=3D"Human">humans</a> on the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moo=
n" title=3D"Moon">Moon</a>, and the fifth crewed mission of <a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASA" title=3D"NASA">NASA</a>'s <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_program" title=3D"Apollo program">Apollo pro=
gram</a>. The mission was crewed by Commander <a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Neil_Armstrong" title=3D"Neil Armstrong">Neil Armstrong</a>, Co=
mmand Module Pilot <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Collins=
_(astronaut)" title=3D"Michael Collins (astronaut)">Michael Collins</a>, an=
d Lunar Module Pilot <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buzz_Aldrin" =
title=3D"Buzz Aldrin">Edwin "Buzz" Aldrin</a>, all of whom were on their se=
cond and final spaceflight.
</p><p>Launched atop a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saturn_V" t=
itle=3D"Saturn V">Saturn V</a> rocket from <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Kennedy_Space_Center" title=3D"Kennedy Space Center">Kennedy Space=
 Center</a> in <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Florida" title=3D"F=
lorida">Florida</a> on July 16 at 13:32 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Coordinated_Universal_Time" title=3D"Coordinated Universal Time">UTC<=
/a>, the Apollo spacecraft consisted of three parts: the <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_command_and_service_module" title=3D"Apollo c=
ommand and service module">command module</a> (CM), which housed the three =
astronauts and was the only part to return to Earth; the <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Service_module" title=3D"Service module">service mod=
ule</a> (SM), which provided propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and wate=
r to the command module; and the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L=
unar_Module" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Lunar Module">Lunar Module</a> =
(LM), which had two stages=E2=80=94a descent stage with a large engine and =
fuel tanks for landing on the Moon, and a lighter ascent stage containing a=
 cabin for two astronauts and a small engine to return them to <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_orbit" title=3D"Lunar orbit">lunar orbit=
</a>. After a three-day transit, Armstrong and Aldrin descended to the surf=
ace aboard the LM <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_Lunar_=
Module_Eagle" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Apollo Lunar Module Eagle">Eag=
le</a></i>, landing in the Sea of Tranquility (<a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Mare_Tranquillitatis" title=3D"Mare Tranquillitatis">Mare Tran=
quillitatis</a>) on July 20 at 20:17 UTC while Collins remained in lunar or=
bit aboard the CM <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Command_modul=
e_Columbia" title=3D"Command module Columbia">Columbia</a></i>. Armstrong b=
ecame the first human to walk on the Moon approximately six hours after lan=
ding, followed by Aldrin nineteen minutes later. Together they spent around=
 two and a half hours walking on the surface, planting an <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_Flag_Assembly" title=3D"Lunar Flag Assembly">=
American flag</a>, speaking by telephone with President <a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Nixon" title=3D"Richard Nixon">Richard Nixon<=
/a>, deploying scientific instruments, and collecting 21.5&nbsp;kg (47.5&nb=
sp;lb) of lunar material. After more than 21 hours on the surface, they rej=
oined Collins in lunar orbit, and the crew returned safely to <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth" title=3D"Earth">Earth</a> on July 24, sp=
lashing down in the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacific_Ocean"=
 title=3D"Pacific Ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>.
</p><p>Apollo 11 was the culmination of the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Space_Race" title=3D"Space Race">Space Race</a>, a geopolitical c=
ompetition between the United States and the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Soviet_Union" title=3D"Soviet Union">Soviet Union</a> rooted in =
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_War" title=3D"Cold War">Cold =
War</a> rivalry. It fulfilled a national goal set by President <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_F._Kennedy" title=3D"John F. Kennedy">Joh=
n F. Kennedy</a> in May 1961, who had <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/We_choose_to_go_to_the_Moon" title=3D"We choose to go to the Moon">chal=
lenged</a> the United States to land a man on the Moon and return him safel=
y before the end of the decade. The program overcame a severe setback with =
the fatal <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_1" title=3D"Apoll=
o 1">Apollo 1</a> launchpad fire in January 1967 and drew on technologies d=
eveloped through the preceding <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pro=
ject_Mercury" title=3D"Project Mercury">Mercury</a> and <a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Gemini" title=3D"Project Gemini">Gemini</a> p=
rograms. The Soviet Union, despite early leads in spaceflight, was unable t=
o match the Saturn V rocket, and its uncrewed lunar probe <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luna_15" title=3D"Luna 15">Luna 15</a> crashed on t=
he Moon on July 21, while Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin were still on the =
lunar surface.
</p><p>Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million =
viewers, roughly one-fifth of the world's population, making it among the m=
ost-watched <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Television" title=3D"T=
elevision">television</a> events in history. Stepping onto the lunar surfac=
e, Armstrong declared: "<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_small_=
step" title=3D"One small step">That's one small step for [a] man, one giant=
 leap for mankind</a>." <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_samp=
le" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Lunar sample">Lunar samples</a> returned=
 by the mission led to the identification of three previously unrecognized =
minerals, and scientific instruments deployed on the surface continued retu=
rning data for years afterwards. The crew were honoured with <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ticker-tape_parade" title=3D"Ticker-tape parade"=
>ticker-tape parades</a>, a world goodwill tour, and the <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidential_Medal_of_Freedom" title=3D"Presidential=
 Medal of Freedom">Presidential Medal of Freedom</a>. The command module <i=
>Columbia</i> is preserved at the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
National_Air_and_Space_Museum" title=3D"National Air and Space Museum">Nati=
onal Air and Space Museum</a> in <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W=
ashington,_D.C." title=3D"Washington, D.C.">Washington, D.C.</a>, while the=
 descent stage of <i>Eagle</i> remains on the lunar surface.
</p>
<meta property=3D"mw:PageProp/toc">
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Background">Background</h2>=
</div>
<p>In the late 1950s and early 1960s, the United States was engaged in the =
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_War" title=3D"Cold War">Cold =
War</a>, a geopolitical rivalry with the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Soviet_Union" title=3D"Soviet Union">Soviet Union</a>.<sup id=3D"cit=
e_ref-FOOTNOTELogsdon1976134_14-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTELogsdon1976134-14"><span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>14<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></=
sup> On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched <i><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sputnik_1" title=3D"Sputnik 1">Sputnik 1</a></i>, the f=
irst <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satellite" title=3D"Satellite=
">artificial satellite</a>, surprising the world and fueling fears about So=
viet technological and military capabilities. Its success demonstrated that=
 the USSR could potentially deliver <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Nuclear_weapon" title=3D"Nuclear weapon">nuclear weapons</a> over <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intercontinental_ballistic_missile" title=
=3D"Intercontinental ballistic missile">intercontinental distances</a>, cha=
llenging American claims of military, economic, and technological superiori=
ty.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTELogsdon197613=E2=80=9315_15-0" class=3D"refe=
rence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOT=
ELogsdon197613%E2%80%9315-15"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>15<span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>This incident sparked the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S=
putnik_crisis" title=3D"Sputnik crisis">Sputnik crisis</a> and ignited the =
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Race" title=3D"Space Race">S=
pace Race</a>, as both superpowers sought to demonstrate superiority in spa=
ceflight.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBrooksGrimwoodSwenson19791_16-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note=
-FOOTNOTEBrooksGrimwoodSwenson19791-16"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</spa=
n>16<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> President <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dwight_D._Eisenhower" title=3D"Dwight D. Eisenh=
ower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a> responded to the challenge posed by Sputnik =
by creating the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASA" title=3D"NAS=
A">National Aeronautics and Space Administration</a> (NASA), and initiating=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Mercury" title=3D"Project=
 Mercury">Project Mercury</a>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTESwensonGrimwoodAl=
exander1966101=E2=80=93106_17-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTESwensonGrimwoodAlexander1966=
101%E2%80%93106-17"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>17<span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> which aimed to place a man into <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geocentric_orbit" title=3D"Geocentric orbit">=
Earth orbit</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTESwensonGrimwoodAlexander1966134=
_18-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_=
11#cite_note-FOOTNOTESwensonGrimwoodAlexander1966134-18"><span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">[</span>18<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The So=
viets took the lead on April 12, 1961, when <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Cosmonaut" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Cosmonaut">cosmonaut</a=
> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuri_Gagarin" title=3D"Yuri Gaga=
rin">Yuri Gagarin</a> became the first person in space and the first to orb=
it the Earth.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTESwensonGrimwoodAlexander1966332=E2=
=80=93333_19-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTESwensonGrimwoodAlexander1966332%E2%80%93333-1=
9"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>19<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</s=
pan></a></sup> Nearly a month later, on May 5, 1961, Alan Shepard became th=
e first American in space; his 15-minute flight was suborbital, not a full =
orbit.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTELogsdon1976121_20-0" class=3D"reference">=
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTELogsdo=
n1976121-20"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>20<span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Because the Soviet Union had <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Launch_vehicle" title=3D"Launch vehicle">launch vehicles</a> with higher =
lift capacity, President John F. Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a c=
hallenge that exceeded the capabilities of the existing generation of rocke=
ts, so that both countries would be starting from an equal position. A crew=
ed mission to the Moon would serve this purpose.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOT=
ELogsdon1976112=E2=80=93117_21-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTELogsdon1976112%E2%80%93117-=
21"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>21<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</=
span></a></sup>
</p><p>On May 25, 1961, Kennedy addressed the <a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/United_States_Congress" title=3D"United States Congress">United=
 States Congress</a> on "Urgent National Needs" and declared:
</p>
<blockquote class=3D"templatequote"><p>I believe that this nation should co=
mmit itself to achieving the goal, before this decade [1960s] is out, of la=
nding a man on the Moon and returning him safely to the Earth. No single sp=
ace project in this period will be more impressive to mankind, or more impo=
rtant for the long-range exploration of space; and none will be so difficul=
t or expensive to accomplish. We propose to accelerate the development of t=
he appropriate lunar space craft. We propose to develop alternate liquid an=
d solid fuel boosters, much larger than any now being developed, until cert=
ain which is superior. We propose additional funds for other engine develop=
ment and for unmanned explorations=E2=80=94explorations which are particula=
rly important for one purpose which this nation will never overlook: the su=
rvival of the man who first makes this daring flight. But in a very real se=
nse, it will not be one man going to the Moon=E2=80=94if we make this judgm=
ent affirmatively, it will be an entire nation. For all of us must work to =
put him there. </p></blockquote><div class=3D"templatequotecite"><p style=
=3D"display: inline; padding-left: 2.3em;">=E2=80=94=E2=80=8AKennedy's spee=
ch to Congress<sup id=3D"cite_ref-22" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-22"><span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">[</span>22<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><span style=3D"d=
isplay:none" data-plural=3D"0"></span></p></div>
<p>On September 12, 1962, Kennedy <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
We_choose_to_go_to_the_Moon" title=3D"We choose to go to the Moon">delivere=
d another speech</a> before a crowd of about 40,000 people in the <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice_Stadium_(Rice_University)" title=3D"=
Rice Stadium (Rice University)">Rice University football stadium</a> in <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Houston" title=3D"Houston">Houston</a=
>, Texas.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-23" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-23"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</=
span>23<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-2=
4" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#c=
ite_note-24"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>24<span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">]</span></a></sup> A widely quoted refrain from the middle portion of=
 the speech reads as follows:
</p>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1244412712"><blockquote class=3D"templatequote"><p>There is no strife, no p=
rejudice, no national conflict in outer space as yet. Its hazards are hosti=
le to us all. Its conquest deserves the best of all mankind, and its opport=
unity for peaceful cooperation may never come again. But why, some say, the=
 Moon? Why choose this as our goal? And they may well ask, why climb the hi=
ghest mountain? Why, 35 years ago, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Spirit_of_St._Louis" title=3D"Spirit of St. Louis">fly the Atlantic</a>? W=
hy does <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice%E2%80%93Texas_footbal=
l_rivalry" title=3D"Rice=E2=80=93Texas football rivalry">Rice play Texas</a=
>?
We choose to go to the Moon! We choose to go to the Moon&nbsp;... We choose=
 to go to the Moon in this decade and do the other things, not because they=
 are easy, but because they are hard; because that goal will serve to organ=
ize and measure the best of our energies and skills, because that challenge=
 is one that we are willing to accept, one we are unwilling to postpone, an=
d one we intend to win, and the others, too.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-25" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note=
-25"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>25<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]<=
/span></a></sup></p></blockquote>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:John_F._Kennedy_speaks_at_Rice_University.jpg=
" class=3D"mw-file-description"><img alt=3D"Kennedy, in a blue suit and tie=
, speaks at a wooden podium bearing the seal of the President of the United=
