<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Preparations">Preparations<=
/h2></div>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Insignia">Insignia</h3></di=
v>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Apollo_11_insignia.png" class=3D"mw-file-desc=
ription"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2=
/27/Apollo_11_insignia.png/250px-Apollo_11_insignia.png" decoding=3D"async"=
 width=3D"250" height=3D"252" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"=
5380" data-file-height=3D"5432"></a><figcaption>The Apollo 11 insignia. The=
 eagle is carrying an olive branch to represent a peaceful mission</figcapt=
ion></figure>
<p>The Apollo 11 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mission_patch" ti=
tle=3D"Mission patch">mission emblem</a> was designed by Collins, who wante=
d a symbol for "peaceful lunar landing by the United States". At Lovell's s=
uggestion, he chose the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bald_eagle=
" title=3D"Bald eagle">bald eagle</a>, the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/National_bird" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"National bird">natio=
nal bird</a> of the United States, as the symbol. Tom Wilson, a simulator i=
nstructor, suggested an <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive_bran=
ch" title=3D"Olive branch">olive branch</a> in its beak to represent their =
peaceful mission. Collins added a lunar background with the Earth in the di=
stance. The sunlight in the image was coming from the wrong direction; the =
shadow should have been in the lower part of the Earth instead of the left.=
<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001332=E2=80=93334_69-0" class=3D"refer=
ence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTE=
Collins2001332%E2%80%93334-69"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>68<span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Aldrin, Armstrong and Collins decided the Eagle and the Moon would b=
e in their natural colors, and decided on a blue and gold border. Armstrong=
 was concerned that "eleven" would not be understood by non-English speaker=
s, so they went with "Apollo 11",<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001332=
=E2=80=93334_69-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollins2001332%E2%80%93334-69"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>68<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>=
 and they decided not to put their names on the patch, so it would "be repr=
esentative of <i>everyone</i> who had worked toward a lunar landing".<sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001332_70-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollins2001332-70"=
><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>69<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</spa=
n></a></sup>
</p><p>An illustrator at the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manne=
d_Spacecraft_Center" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Manned Spacecraft Cente=
r">Manned Spacecraft Center</a> (MSC) did the artwork, which was then sent =
off to NASA officials for approval.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins20013=
32=E2=80=93334_69-2" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollins2001332%E2%80%93334-69"><span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>68<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></su=
p> The design was rejected. <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bob_Gi=
lruth" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Bob Gilruth">Bob Gilruth</a>, the dir=
ector of the MSC felt the talons of the eagle looked "too warlike".<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001333_71-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollins2001333-71">=
<span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>70<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span=
></a></sup> After some discussion, the olive branch was moved to the talons=
.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001333_71-1" class=3D"reference"><a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollins2001=
333-71"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>70<span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>]</span></a></sup> When the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eisen=
hower_dollar" title=3D"Eisenhower dollar">Eisenhower dollar coin</a> was re=
leased in 1971, the patch design provided the eagle for its reverse side.<s=
up id=3D"cite_ref-72" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-72"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>71<spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The design was also used for th=
e smaller <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susan_B._Anthony_dollar"=
 title=3D"Susan B. Anthony dollar">Susan B. Anthony dollar</a> unveiled in =
1979.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-73" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-73"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span=
>72<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Call_signs">Call signs</h3>=
</div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:NASM-NASM2013-02663.jpg" class=3D"mw-file-des=
cription"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/=
d/dc/NASM-NASM2013-02663.jpg/250px-NASM-NASM2013-02663.jpg" decoding=3D"asy=
nc" width=3D"250" height=3D"188" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=
=3D"12000" data-file-height=3D"9000"></a><figcaption>The original cockpit o=
f the command module (CM) with three seats, photographed from above. It is =
located in the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Air_and_Sp=
ace_Museum" title=3D"National Air and Space Museum">National Air and Space =
Museum</a>. The very high resolution image was produced in 2007 by the <a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smithsonian_Institution" title=3D"Smit=
hsonian Institution">Smithsonian Institution</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<p>After the crew of Apollo 10 named their spacecraft <i>Charlie Brown</i> =
and <i>Snoopy</i>, assistant manager for public affairs <a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_Scheer" title=3D"Julian Scheer">Julian Scheer<=
/a> wrote to <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Low" title=3D"=
George Low">George Low</a>, the Manager of the Apollo Spacecraft Program Of=
fice at the MSC, to suggest the Apollo 11 crew be less flippant in naming t=
heir craft. The name <i>Snowcone</i> was used for the CM and <i>Haystack</i=
> was used for the LM in both internal and external communications during e=
arly mission planning.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMarshall_Space_Flight_Cen=
ter19698_74-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMarshall_Space_Flight_Center19698-74"><span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>73<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></s=
up>
</p><p>The LM was named <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_M=
odule_Eagle" title=3D"Lunar Module Eagle">Eagle</a></i> after the motif whi=
ch was featured prominently on the mission insignia. At Scheer's suggestion=
, the CM was named <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Command_modu=
le_Columbia" title=3D"Command module Columbia">Columbia</a></i> after <i><a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbiad#In_fiction" title=3D"Colum=
biad">Columbiad</a></i>, the giant cannon that launched a spacecraft (also =
from Florida) in <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jules_Verne" titl=
e=3D"Jules Verne">Jules Verne</a>'s 1865 novel <i><a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/From_the_Earth_to_the_Moon" title=3D"From the Earth to the =
Moon">From the Earth to the Moon</a></i>. It also referred to <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbia_(name)" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D=
"Columbia (name)">Columbia</a>, a historical name of the United States.<sup=
 id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001334=E2=80=93335_75-0" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEColl=
ins2001334%E2%80%93335-75"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>74<span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBrooksGri=
mwoodSwenson1979331_76-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBrooksGrimwoodSwenson1979331-76"><s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>75<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span><=
/a></sup> In Collins' 1976 book, he said <i>Columbia</i> was in reference t=
o <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christopher_Columbus" title=3D"C=
hristopher Columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEC=
ollins1994116_77-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollins1994116-77"><span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">[</span>76<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Mementos">Mementos</h3></di=
v>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
