edia.org/wiki/NASA_space-flown_Gemini_and_Apollo_medallions" title=3D"NASA =
space-flown Gemini and Apollo medallions">Robbins medallion</a></figcaption=
></figure>
<p>The astronauts had <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_pre=
ference_kit" title=3D"Personal preference kit">personal preference kits</a>=
 (PPKs), small bags containing personal items of significance they wanted t=
o take with them on the mission.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-78" class=3D"reference"=
><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-78"><span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>77<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></su=
p> Five 0.5-pound (0.23&nbsp;kg) PPKs were carried on Apollo 11: three (one=
 for each astronaut) were stowed on <i>Columbia</i> before launch, and two =
on <i>Eagle</i>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-79" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-79"><span class=3D"cite-brack=
et">[</span>78<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Neil Armstrong's LM PPK contained a piece of wood from the <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wright_brothers" title=3D"Wright brothers=
">Wright brothers</a>' 1903 <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wri=
ght_Flyer" title=3D"Wright Flyer">Wright Flyer</a></i><span class=3D"nowrap=
" style=3D"padding-left:0.1em;">'</span>s left propeller and a piece of fab=
ric from its wing,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHansen2005527_80-0" class=3D"=
reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOO=
TNOTEHansen2005527-80"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>79<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> along with a diamond-studded <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astronaut_pin" class=3D"mw-redirect" titl=
e=3D"Astronaut pin">astronaut pin</a> originally given to Slayton by the wi=
dows of the Apollo&nbsp;1 crew. This pin had been intended to be flown on t=
hat mission and given to Slayton afterwards, but following the disastrous l=
aunch pad fire and subsequent funerals, the widows gave the pin to Slayton.=
 Armstrong took it with him on Apollo 11.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTESlayto=
nCassutt1994191=E2=80=93192_81-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTESlaytonCassutt1994191%E2%80=
%93192-81"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>80<span class=3D"cite-brack=
et">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
