<p>NASA's Apollo Site Selection Board announced five potential landing site=
s on February 8, 1968. These were the result of two years' worth of studies=
 based on high-resolution photography of the lunar surface by the five uncr=
ewed probes of the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_Orbiter_p=
rogram" title=3D"Lunar Orbiter program">Lunar Orbiter program</a> and infor=
mation about surface conditions provided by the <a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Surveyor_program" title=3D"Surveyor program">Surveyor program=
</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Site_Selection_82-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-Site_Selection-82"><s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>81<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span><=
/a></sup> The best Earth-bound telescopes could not resolve features with t=
he resolution Project Apollo required.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECortright=
197579_83-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A=
pollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECortright197579-83"><span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>[</span>82<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The landing site=
 had to be close to the lunar equator to minimize the amount of propellant =
required, clear of obstacles to minimize maneuvering, and flat to simplify =
the task of the landing radar. Scientific value was not a consideration.<su=
p id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHarland199919_84-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHarland199919-84=
"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>83<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</sp=
an></a></sup>
</p><p>Areas that appeared promising on photographs taken on Earth were oft=
en found to be totally unacceptable. The original requirement that the site=
 be free of craters had to be relaxed, as no such site was found.<sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECortright197598=E2=80=9399_85-0" class=3D"reference"><a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECortright1=
97598%E2%80%9399-85"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>84<span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Five sites were considered: Sites&nbsp;1 a=
nd&nbsp;2 were in the Sea of Tranquility (<i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Mare_Tranquillitatis" title=3D"Mare Tranquillitatis">Mare Tranqu=
illitatis</a></i>); Site&nbsp;3 was in the Central Bay (<span style=3D"padd=
ing-right:0.15em;"><i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinus_Medii"=
 title=3D"Sinus Medii">Sinus Medii</a></i></span>); and Sites&nbsp;4 and&nb=
sp;5 were in the Ocean of Storms (<i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Oceanus_Procellarum" title=3D"Oceanus Procellarum">Oceanus Procellarum</=
a></i>).<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Site_Selection_82-1" class=3D"reference"><a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-Site_Selection-82"><=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>81<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup>
The final site selection was based on seven criteria:
</p>
<ul><li>The site needed to be smooth, with relatively few craters;</li>
<li>with approach paths free of large hills, tall cliffs or deep craters th=
at might confuse the landing radar and cause it to issue incorrect readings=
;</li>
<li>reachable with a minimum amount of propellant;</li>
<li>allowing for delays in the launch countdown;</li>
<li>providing the Apollo spacecraft with a free-return trajectory, one that=
 would allow it to coast around the Moon and safely return to Earth without=
 requiring any engine firings should a problem arise on the way to the Moon=
;</li>
<li>with good visibility during the landing approach, meaning the Sun would=
 be between 7&nbsp;and 20 degrees behind the LM; and</li>
<li>a general slope of less than two degrees in the landing area.<sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-Site_Selection_82-2" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-Site_Selection-82"><span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">[</span>81<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></li></u=
l>
<p>The requirement for the Sun angle was particularly restrictive, limiting=
 the launch date to one day per month.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Site_Selection_82=
-3" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#=
cite_note-Site_Selection-82"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>81<span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> A landing just after dawn was chos=
en to limit the temperature extremes the astronauts would experience.<sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins19947_86-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollins19947-86"><sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>85<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></=
a></sup> The Apollo Site Selection Board selected Site&nbsp;2, with Sites 3=
&nbsp;and&nbsp;5 as backups in the event of the launch being delayed. In Ma=
y 1969, Apollo 10's lunar module flew to within 15 kilometers (9.3&nbsp;mi)=
 of Site&nbsp;2, and reported it was acceptable.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOT=
ECappellari1972976_87-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECappellari1972976-87"><span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">[</span>86<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><su=
p id=3D"cite_ref-88" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-88"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>87<span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"First-step_decision">First-=
step decision</h3></div>
<p>During the first press conference after the Apollo 11 crew was announced=
, the first question was, "Which one of you gentlemen will be the first man=
 to step onto the lunar surface?"<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChaikin1994148=
_89-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_=
11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChaikin1994148-89"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</spa=
n>88<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOT=
NOTEHansen2005360_90-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHansen2005360-90"><span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">[</span>89<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Slayton=
 told the reporter it had not been decided, and Armstrong added that it was=
 "not based on individual desire".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChaikin199414=
8_89-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo=
_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChaikin1994148-89"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>88<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>One of the first versions of the egress checklist had the lunar modu=
le pilot exit the spacecraft before the commander, which matched what had b=
een done on Gemini missions,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001347_91-0=
" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#ci=
te_note-FOOTNOTECollins2001347-91"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>90<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> where the commander had neve=
r performed the spacewalk.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEAldrinAbraham201657=
=E2=80=9358_92-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEAldrinAbraham201657%E2%80%9358-92"><span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>91<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></s=
up> Reporters wrote in early 1969 that Aldrin would be the first man to wal=
k on the Moon, and Associate Administrator <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/George_Mueller_(NASA)" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"George Muell=
er (NASA)">George Mueller</a> told reporters he would be first as well. Ald=
rin heard that Armstrong would be the first because Armstrong was a civilia=
n, which made Aldrin livid. Aldrin attempted to persuade other Lunar Module=
 pilots he should be first, but they responded cynically about what they pe=
rceived as a lobbying campaign. Attempting to stem interdepartmental confli=
ct, Slayton told Aldrin that Armstrong would be first since he was the comm=
ander. The decision was announced in a press conference on April 14, 1969.<=
sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHansen2005363=E2=80=93365_93-0" class=3D"referen=
ce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHa=
nsen2005363%E2%80%93365-93"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>92<span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>For decades, Aldrin believed the final decision was largely driven b=
y the Lunar Module's hatch location. Because the astronauts had their space=
suits on and the spacecraft was so small, maneuvering to exit the spacecraf=
t was difficult. The crew tried a simulation in which Aldrin left the space=
craft first, but he damaged the simulator while attempting to egress. While=
 this was enough for mission planners to make their decision, Aldrin and Ar=
mstrong were left in the dark on the decision until late spring.<sup id=3D"=
cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChaikin1994149_94-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChaikin1994149-94"><spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>93<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a=
></sup> Slayton told Armstrong the plan was to have him leave the spacecraf=
t first, if he agreed. Armstrong said, "Yes, that's the way to do it."<sup =
id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChaikin1994150_95-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChaikin1994150-95=
"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>94<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</sp=
an></a></sup>
</p><p>The media accused Armstrong of exercising his commander's prerogativ=
e to exit the spacecraft first.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTESchefter1999281_=
96-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_1=
1#cite_note-FOOTNOTESchefter1999281-96"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</spa=
n>95<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Chris_Kraft" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Chris Kraft">=
Chris Kraft</a> revealed in his 2001 autobiography that a meeting occurred =
between Gilruth, Slayton, Low, and himself to make sure Aldrin would not be=
 the first to walk on the Moon. They argued that the first person to walk o=
n the Moon should be like <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_=
Lindbergh" title=3D"Charles Lindbergh">Charles Lindbergh</a>, a calm and qu=
iet person. They made the decision to change the flight plan so the command=
er was the first to egress from the spacecraft.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTE=
Hansen2005371=E2=80=93372_97-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHansen2005371%E2%80%93372-97"=
><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>96<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</spa=
n></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Pre-launch">Pre-launch</h3>=
</div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Apollo_11_Saturn_V_SN_SA506_(6=
9-HC-620).jpg" class=3D"mw-file-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wik=
imedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Apollo_11_Saturn_V_SN_SA506_%2869-H=
C-620%29.jpg/330px-Apollo_11_Saturn_V_SN_SA506_%2869-HC-620%29.jpg" decodin=
g=3D"async" width=3D"330" height=3D"252" style=3D"--mw-file-upright: 1.3" c=
lass=3D"mw-file-element mw-file-upright" data-file-width=3D"1200" data-file=
-height=3D"917"></a><figcaption>Saturn V SA-506, the rocket carrying the Ap=
ollo 11 spacecraft, moves out of the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Vehicle_Assembly_Building" title=3D"Vehicle Assembly Building">Vehicle A=
ssembly Building</a> towards <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Launc=
h_Complex_39" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Launch Complex 39">Launch Comp=
lex 39</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The ascent stage of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_Modul=
e_Eagle" title=3D"Lunar Module Eagle">LM-5 <i>Eagle</i></a> arrived at the =
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kennedy_Space_Center" title=3D"Ken=
nedy Space Center">Kennedy Space Center</a> on January 8, 1969, followed by=
 the descent stage four days later, and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Command_module_Columbia" title=3D"Command module Columbia">CSM-107 <i=
>Columbia</i></a> on January 23.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Mission_Overview_6-1" c=
lass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_=
note-Mission_Overview-6"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>6<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> There were several differences between=
 <i>Eagle</i> and Apollo 10's LM-4 <i>Snoopy</i>; <i>Eagle</i> had a VHF ra=
dio antenna to facilitate communication with the astronauts during their EV=
A on the lunar surface; a lighter ascent engine; more thermal protection on=
 the landing gear; and a package of scientific experiments known as the <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Early_Apollo_Scientific_Experiments_P=
ackage" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Early Apollo Scientific Experiments =
Package">Early Apollo Scientific Experiments Package</a> (EASEP). The only =
change in the configuration of the command module was the removal of some i=
nsulation from the forward hatch.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBensonFaherty1=
978472_98-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A=
pollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBensonFaherty1978472-98"><span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">[</span>97<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"c=
ite_ref-99" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ap=
ollo_11#cite_note-99"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>98<span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The CSM was mated on January 29, and move=
d from the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operations_and_Checkout=
_Building" title=3D"Operations and Checkout Building">Operations and Checko=
ut Building</a> to the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vehicle_Ass=
embly_Building" title=3D"Vehicle Assembly Building">Vehicle Assembly Buildi=
ng</a> on April 14.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Mission_Overview_6-2" class=3D"refer=
ence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-Mission_=
Overview-6"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>6<span class=3D"cite-brack=
et">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>The <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S-IVB" title=3D"S-IVB">=
S-IVB</a> third stage of Saturn V AS-506 had arrived on January 18, followe=
d by the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S-II" title=3D"S-II">S-II=
</a> second stage on February 6, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S=
-IC" title=3D"S-IC">S-IC</a> first stage on February 20, and the <a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saturn_V_Instrument_Unit" class=3D"mw-redire=
ct" title=3D"Saturn V Instrument Unit">Saturn V Instrument Unit</a> on Febr=
uary 27. At 12:30 on May 20, the 5,443-tonne (5,357-long-ton; 6,000-short-t=
on) assembly departed the Vehicle Assembly Building atop the <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crawler-transporter" title=3D"Crawler-transporte=
r">crawler-transporter</a>, bound for Launch Pad 39A, part of <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Launch_Complex_39" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=
=3D"Launch Complex 39">Launch Complex 39</a>, while Apollo 10 was still on =
its way to the Moon. A countdown test commenced on June 26, and concluded o=
n July 2. The launch complex was floodlit on the night of July 15, when the=
 crawler-transporter carried the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S=
ervice_structure" title=3D"Service structure">mobile service structure</a> =
back to its parking area.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Mission_Overview_6-3" class=3D=
"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-Mi=
ssion_Overview-6"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>6<span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">]</span></a></sup> In the early hours of the morning, the fuel ta=
nks of the S-II and S-IVB stages were filled with <a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Liquid_hydrogen" title=3D"Liquid hydrogen">liquid hydrogen<=
/a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBensonFaherty1978474_100-0" class=3D"refere=
nce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEB=
ensonFaherty1978474-100"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>99<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Fueling was completed by three hours b=
efore launch.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBensonFaherty1978475_101-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note=
-FOOTNOTEBensonFaherty1978475-101"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>100=
<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Launch operations were part=
ly automated, with 43 programs written in the <a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/ATOLL_(programming_language)" title=3D"ATOLL (programming langu=
age)">ATOLL programming language</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBensonFahe=
rty1978355=E2=80=93356_102-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBensonFaherty1978355%E2%80%9335=
6-102"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>101<span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Slayton roused the crew shortly after 04:00, and they showered, shav=
ed, and had the traditional pre-flight breakfast of steak and eggs with Sla=
yton and the backup crew. They then donned their space suits and began brea=
thing pure oxygen. At 06:30, they headed out to Launch Complex 39.<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001355=E2=80=93357_103-0" class=3D"reference">=
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollin=
s2001355%E2%80%93357-103"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>102<span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Haise entered <i>Columbia</i> about =
three hours and ten minutes before launch time. Along with a technician, he=
 helped Armstrong into the left-hand couch at 06:54. Five minutes later, Co=
llins joined him, taking up his position on the right-hand couch. Finally, =
Aldrin entered, taking the center couch.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBensonF=
aherty1978475_101-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBensonFaherty1978475-101"><span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">[</span>100<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> H=
aise left around two hours and ten minutes before launch.<sup id=3D"cite_re=
f-AP11FJ_104-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Apollo_11#cite_note-AP11FJ-104"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>103<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The closeout crew sealed the=
 hatch, and the cabin was purged and pressurized. The closeout crew then le=
ft the launch complex about an hour before launch time. The countdown becam=
e automated at three minutes and twenty seconds before launch time.<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBensonFaherty1978475_101-2" class=3D"reference"><a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBensonFahert=
y1978475-101"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>100<span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">]</span></a></sup> Over 450 personnel were at the consoles in the <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firing_room" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
title=3D"Firing room">firing room</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBensonFah=
erty1978474_100-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBensonFaherty1978474-100"><span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">[</span>99<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Mission">Mission</h2></div>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Launch_and_flight_to_lunar_=
orbit">Launch and flight to lunar orbit</h3></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Apollo_11_Launch_-_GPN-2000-000630.jpg" class=
=3D"mw-file-description"><img alt=3D"" src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/=
wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Apollo_11_Launch_-_GPN-2000-000630.jpg/250px-A=
pollo_11_Launch_-_GPN-2000-000630.jpg" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"250" hei=
ght=3D"313" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"2400" data-file-he=
ight=3D"3000"></a><figcaption>The Apollo 11 Saturn V space vehicle lifts of=
f with astronauts Neil A. Armstrong, Michael Collins and Edwin E. Aldrin Jr=
. at 9:32&nbsp;am. EDT July 16, 1969, from Kennedy Space Center's Launch Co=
mplex 39A.</figcaption></figure>
<p>An estimated one million spectators watched the launch of Apollo 11 from=
 the highways and beaches in the vicinity of the launch site. Dignitaries i=
ncluded the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_of_Staff_of_the_=
United_States_Army" title=3D"Chief of Staff of the United States Army">Chie=
f of Staff of the United States Army</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/General_(United_States)" title=3D"General (United States)">General<=
/a> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Westmoreland" title=3D=
"William Westmoreland">William Westmoreland</a>, four <a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabinet_of_the_United_States" title=3D"Cabinet of the U=
nited States">cabinet members</a>, 19 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Governor_(United_States)" title=3D"Governor (United States)">state gove=
rnors</a>, 40 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mayoralty_in_the_Uni=
ted_States" title=3D"Mayoralty in the United States">mayors</a>, 60 ambassa=
dors and 200 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congressmen" class=3D=
"mw-redirect" title=3D"Congressmen">congressmen</a>. Vice President <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spiro_Agnew" title=3D"Spiro Agnew">Spiro =
Agnew</a> viewed the launch with former president <a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Lyndon_B._Johnson" title=3D"Lyndon B. Johnson">Lyndon B. Jo=
hnson</a> and his wife <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady_Bird_J=
ohnson" title=3D"Lady Bird Johnson">Lady Bird Johnson</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_r=
ef-FOOTNOTEBensonFaherty1978474_100-2" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBensonFaherty1978474-1=
00"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>99<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</=
span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBilstein1980369=E2=80=93370_105-=
0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#c=
ite_note-FOOTNOTEBilstein1980369%E2%80%93370-105"><span class=3D"cite-brack=
et">[</span>104<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Around 3,500=
 media representatives were present.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBrooksGrimw=
oodSwenson1979338_106-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBrooksGrimwoodSwenson1979338-106"><s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>105<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup> About two-thirds were from the United States; the rest came from=
 55 other countries. The launch was televised live in 33 countries, with an=
 estimated 25&nbsp;million viewers in the United States alone. Millions mor=
e around the world listened to radio broadcasts.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOT=
EBilstein1980369=E2=80=93370_105-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBilstein1980369%E2%80%933=
70-105"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>104<span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBensonFaherty1978474_100-3"=
 class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cit=
e_note-FOOTNOTEBensonFaherty1978474-100"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>99<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> President <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Nixon" title=3D"Richard Nixon">Richard=
 Nixon</a> viewed the launch from his office in the <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/White_House" title=3D"White House">White House</a> with h=
is NASA liaison officer, Apollo astronaut <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Frank_Borman" title=3D"Frank Borman">Frank Borman</a>.<sup id=3D"ci=
te_ref-107" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ap=
ollo_11#cite_note-107"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>106<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Lodging near Cape Canaveral was report=
ed as being booked months ahead in advance for the launch by a Florida news=
paper.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-108" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-108"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</s=
pan>107<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Saturn V AS-506 launched Apollo 11 on July 16, 1969, at 13:32:00 <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UTC" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"U=
TC">UTC</a> (9:32:00 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Dayli=
ght_Time" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Eastern Daylight Time">EDT</a>).<s=
up id=3D"cite_ref-Mission_Overview_6-4" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-Mission_Overview-6"><span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>6<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
> At 13.2 seconds into the flight, the vehicle began to <a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roll_program" title=3D"Roll program">roll</a> into it=
s <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flight_azimuth" class=3D"mw-redi=
rect" title=3D"Flight azimuth">flight azimuth</a> of 72.058=C2=B0. Full shu=
tdown of the first-stage engines occurred about 2&nbsp;minutes and 42 secon=
ds into the mission, followed by separation of the S-IC and ignition of the=
 S-II engines. The second stage engines then cut off and separated at about=
 9&nbsp;minutes and 8&nbsp;seconds, allowing the first ignition of the S-IV=
B engine a few seconds later.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000106_8-2"=
 class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cit=
e_note-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000106-8"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>8<span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Apollo 11 entered a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circula=
r_orbit" title=3D"Circular orbit">near-circular Earth orbit</a> at an altit=
ude of 100.4 nautical miles (185.9&nbsp;km) by 98.9 nautical miles (183.2&n=
bsp;km), twelve minutes into its flight. After one and a half orbits, which=
 took around two and a half hours, a second ignition of the S-IVB engine pu=
shed the spacecraft onto its trajectory toward the Moon with the <a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trans-lunar_injection" title=3D"Trans-lunar =
injection">trans-lunar injection</a> (TLI) burn, which took about 5 minutes=
 and 47 seconds and started at 16:16:16 UTC. About 30 minutes later, with C=
ollins in the left seat and at the controls, the <a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Transposition,_docking,_and_extraction" title=3D"Transpositi=
on, docking, and extraction">transposition, docking, and extraction</a> man=
euver was performed. This involved separating <i>Columbia</i> from the spen=
t S-IVB stage, turning around, and docking with <i>Eagle</i> still attached=
 to the stage. After the LM was extracted, the combined spacecraft headed f=
or the Moon, while the rocket stage flew on a trajectory past the Moon.<sup=
 id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001374=E2=80=93375_109-0" class=3D"referenc=
e"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECol=
lins2001374%E2%80%93375-109"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>108<span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEOrloff=
2000106_8-3" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A=
pollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000106-8"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[<=
/span>8<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> This was done to avo=
id the third stage colliding with the spacecraft, the Earth, or the Moon. A=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravity_assist" title=3D"Gravity =
assist">slingshot effect</a> from passing around the Moon threw it into an =
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heliocentric_orbit" title=3D"Helio=
centric orbit">orbit around the Sun</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMarshal=
l_Space_Flight_Center19697_110-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMarshall_Space_Flight_Cente=
r19697-110"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>109<span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>On July 19 at 17:21:50 UTC, Apollo 11 passed behind the Moon and fir=
ed its service propulsion engine to enter <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Lunar_orbit" title=3D"Lunar orbit">lunar orbit</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_=
ref-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000106_8-4" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000106-8"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>8<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><=
sup id=3D"cite_ref-111" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-111"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>110=
<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> In the thirty orbits that f=
ollowed, the crew saw passing views of their landing site in the southern S=
ea of Tranquility about 12 miles (19&nbsp;km) southwest of the crater <a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collins_(crater)" title=3D"Collins (cra=
ter)">Sabine D</a>. The site was selected in part because it had been chara=
cterized as relatively flat and smooth by the automated <a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranger_8" title=3D"Ranger 8">Ranger 8</a> and <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surveyor_5" title=3D"Surveyor 5">Surveyor=
 5</a> landers and the Lunar Orbiter mapping spacecraft, and because it was=
 unlikely to present major landing or EVA challenges.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-11=
2" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#c=
ite_note-112"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>111<span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">]</span></a></sup> It lay about 25 kilometers (16&nbsp;mi) southeas=
t of the Surveyor&nbsp;5 landing site, and 68 kilometers (42&nbsp;mi) south=
west of Ranger&nbsp;8's crash site.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMission_Eval=
uation_Team1969130_113-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMission_Evaluation_Team1969130-113"=
><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>112<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</sp=
an></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Lunar_descent">Lunar descen=
t</h3></div>
