(AS11-37-5445).jpg" class=3D"mw-file-description"><img alt=3D"The top of th=
e silvery command module is seen over a grey, cratered lunar surface" src=
=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Apollo_11_CSM=
_photographed_from_Lunar_Module_%28AS11-37-5445%29.jpg/330px-Apollo_11_CSM_=
photographed_from_Lunar_Module_%28AS11-37-5445%29.jpg" decoding=3D"async" w=
idth=3D"300" height=3D"300" style=3D"--mw-file-upright: 1.2" class=3D"mw-fi=
le-element mw-file-upright" data-file-width=3D"4112" data-file-height=3D"41=
12"></a><figcaption><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Command_module=
_Columbia" title=3D"Command module Columbia"><i>Columbia</i></a> in lunar o=
rbit, photographed from <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_Modu=
le_Eagle" title=3D"Lunar Module Eagle"><i>Eagle</i></a></figcaption></figur=
e>
<p>At 12:52:00 UTC on July 20, Aldrin and Armstrong entered <a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_Module_Eagle" title=3D"Lunar Module Eagle">=
<i>Eagle</i></a>, and began the final preparations for lunar descent.<sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000106_8-5" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000106-8"><sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>8<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a=
></sup> At 17:44:00 <i>Eagle</i> separated from <i>Columbia</i>.<sup id=3D"=
cite_ref-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000107_13-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000107-13"><span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>13<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a><=
/sup> Collins, alone aboard <i>Columbia</i>, inspected <i>Eagle</i> as it p=
irouetted before him to ensure the craft was not damaged, and that the land=
ing gear was correctly deployed.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMission_Evaluat=
ion_Team19699_114-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMission_Evaluation_Team19699-114"><span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>113<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a>=
</sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollinsAldrin1975209_115-0" class=3D"refe=
rence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOT=
ECollinsAldrin1975209-115"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>114<span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Armstrong exclaimed: "The <i>Eagle<=
/i> has wings!"<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollinsAldrin1975209_115-1" clas=
s=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_not=
e-FOOTNOTECollinsAldrin1975209-115"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>11=
4<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>As the descent began, Armstrong and Aldrin found themselves passing =
landmarks on the surface two or three seconds early, and reported that they=
 were "long"; they would land miles west of their target point. <i>Eagle</i=
> was traveling too fast. The problem could have been <a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mass_concentration_(astronomy)" title=3D"Mass concentra=
tion (astronomy)">mascons</a>=E2=80=94concen=C2=ADtra=C2=ADtions of high ma=
ss in a region or regions of the Moon's crust that contains a <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravity_anomaly" title=3D"Gravity anomaly">grav=
itational anomaly</a>, potentially altering <i>Eagle</i><span class=3D"nowr=
ap" style=3D"padding-left:0.1em;">'s</span> trajectory. Flight Director Gen=
e Kranz speculated that it could have resulted from extra air pressure in t=
he docking tunnel, or a result of <i>Eagle</i><span class=3D"nowrap" style=
=3D"padding-left:0.1em;">'</span>s pirouette maneuver.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-F=
OOTNOTEMindell2008220=E2=80=93221_116-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMindell2008220%E2%80=
%93221-116"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>115<span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMission_Evaluation_Team=
196982_117-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMission_Evaluation_Team196982-117"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>116<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
>
</p><p>Five minutes into the descent burn, and 6,000 feet (1,800&nbsp;m) ab=
ove the surface of the Moon, the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A=
pollo_Guidance_Computer" title=3D"Apollo Guidance Computer">LM guidance com=
puter</a> (LGC) distracted the crew with the first of several unexpected 12=
01 and 1202 program alarms. Inside Mission Control Center, computer enginee=
r <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Garman" title=3D"Jack Garma=
n">Jack Garman</a> told <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flight_con=
troller#GUIDO" title=3D"Flight controller">Guidance Officer</a> <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_Bales" title=3D"Steve Bales">Steve Bale=
s</a> it was safe to continue the descent, and this was relayed to the crew=
. The program alarms indicated "executive overflows", meaning the guidance =
computer could not complete all its tasks in real-time and had to postpone =
some of them.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollinsAldrin1975210=E2=80=93212_1=
18-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_1=
1#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollinsAldrin1975210%E2%80%93212-118"><span class=3D"ci=
te-bracket">[</span>117<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup =
id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHamiltonHackler200834=E2=80=9343_119-0" class=3D"ref=
erence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNO=
TEHamiltonHackler200834%E2%80%9343-119"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</spa=
n>118<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_Hamilton_(software_engineer)" title=3D"Margaret=
 Hamilton (software engineer)">Margaret Hamilton</a>, the Director of Apoll=
o Flight Computer Programming at the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/MIT" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"MIT">MIT</a> <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Stark_Draper_Laboratory" class=3D"mw-redirect" ti=
tle=3D"Charles Stark Draper Laboratory">Charles Stark Draper Laboratory</a>=
 later recalled:
</p>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Apollo_11_Lunar_Module_Eagle_in_landing_confi=
guration_in_lunar_orbit_from_the_Command_and_Service_Module_Columbia.jpg" c=
lass=3D"mw-file-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikip=
edia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Apollo_11_Lunar_Module_Eagle_in_landing_configurati=
on_in_lunar_orbit_from_the_Command_and_Service_Module_Columbia.jpg/330px-Ap=
ollo_11_Lunar_Module_Eagle_in_landing_configuration_in_lunar_orbit_from_the=
_Command_and_Service_Module_Columbia.jpg" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"310" =
height=3D"279" style=3D"--mw-file-upright: 1.25" class=3D"mw-file-element m=
w-file-upright" data-file-width=3D"2480" data-file-height=3D"2229"></a><fig=
caption><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_Module_Eagle" title=
=3D"Lunar Module Eagle"><i>Eagle</i></a> in <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Lunar_orbit" title=3D"Lunar orbit">lunar orbit</a> photographed f=
rom <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Command_module_Columbia" title=
=3D"Command module Columbia"><i>Columbia</i></a></figcaption></figure>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1244412712"><blockquote class=3D"templatequote"><p>To blame the computer fo=
r the Apollo 11 problems is like blaming the person who spots a fire and ca=
lls the fire department. Actually, the computer was programmed to do more t=
han recognize error conditions. A complete set of recovery programs was inc=
orporated into the software. The software's action, in this case, was to el=
iminate lower priority tasks and re-establish the more important ones. The =
computer, rather than almost forcing an abort, prevented an abort. If the c=
omputer hadn't recognized this problem and taken recovery action, I doubt i=
f Apollo 11 would have been the successful Moon landing it was.<sup id=3D"c=
ite_ref-120" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A=
pollo_11#cite_note-120"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>119<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></p></blockquote>
<p>During the mission, the cause was diagnosed as the rendezvous radar swit=
ch being in the wrong position, causing the computer to process data from b=
oth the rendezvous and landing radars at the same time.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-=
FOOTNOTEMission_Evaluation_Team1969190=E2=80=93192_121-0" class=3D"referenc=
e"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMis=
sion_Evaluation_Team1969190%E2%80%93192-121"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[=
</span>120<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_re=
f-122" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_=
11#cite_note-122"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>121<span class=3D"ci=
te-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Software engineer <a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Don_Eyles" title=3D"Don Eyles">Don Eyles</a> concluded in a =
2005 Guidance and Control Conference paper that the problem was due to a ha=
rdware design bug previously seen during testing of the first uncrewed LM i=
n <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_5" title=3D"Apollo 5">Apo=
llo 5</a>. Having the rendezvous radar on (so it was warmed up in case of a=
n emergency landing abort) should have been irrelevant to the computer, but=
 an electrical phasing mismatch between two parts of the rendezvous radar s=
ystem could cause the stationary antenna to appear to the computer as dithe=
ring back and forth between two positions, depending upon how the hardware =
randomly powered up. The extra spurious <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Cycle_stealing" title=3D"Cycle stealing">cycle stealing</a>, as the r=
endezvous radar updated an involuntary counter, caused the computer alarms.=
<sup id=3D"cite_ref-123" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-123"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>12=
2<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Landing">Landing</h3></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><span><span clas=
s=3D"mw-tmh-player video"><video id=3D"mwe_player_0_placeholder" poster=3D"=
