.org/wiki/File:AP11_FINAL_APPROACH.ogv" class=3D"mw-file-magnify"></a><figc=
aption>Armstrong pilots <i>Eagle</i> to its landing on the Moon, July 20, 1=
969.</figcaption></figure>
<p>When Armstrong again looked outside, he saw that the computer's landing =
system was targeting a boulder-strewn area just north and east of a 300-foo=
t-diameter (91&nbsp;m) crater (later determined to be <a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_(lunar_crater)" title=3D"West (lunar crater)">West=
 crater</a>), so he took semi-automatic control.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOT=
EChaikin1994196_124-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChaikin1994196-124"><span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">[</span>123<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMindell2008195=E2=80=93197_125-0" class=3D"reference"=
><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMinde=
ll2008195%E2%80%93197-125"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>124<span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Armstrong considered landing short =
of the boulder field so they could collect geological samples from it, but =
could not since their horizontal velocity was too high. Throughout the desc=
ent, Aldrin called out navigation data to Armstrong, who was busy piloting =
<i>Eagle</i>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChaikin1994197_126-0" class=3D"ref=
erence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNO=
TEChaikin1994197-126"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>125<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Armstrong found a clear patch of ground and maneuvered the spacecraf=
t towards it. As he got closer, now 250 feet (76&nbsp;m) above the surface,=
 he discovered his new landing site had a crater in it.  Now 107 feet (33&n=
bsp;m) above the surface, Armstrong knew their propellant supply was dwindl=
ing and was determined to land at the first possible landing site. He clear=
ed the crater and found another patch of level ground. They were now 100 fe=
et (30&nbsp;m) from the surface, with only 90 seconds of propellant remaini=
ng. Lunar dust kicked up by the LM's engine began to impair his ability to =
determine the spacecraft's motion. Some large rocks jutted out of the dust =
cloud, and Armstrong focused on them during his descent so he could determi=
ne the spacecraft's speed.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChaikin1994197=E2=80=
=93199_127-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChaikin1994197%E2%80%93199-127"><span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">[</span>126<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>A light informed Aldrin that at least one of the 67-inch (170&nbsp;c=
m) probes hanging from <i>Eagle</i><span class=3D"nowrap" style=3D"padding-=
left:0.1em;">'s</span> footpads had touched the surface a few moments befor=
e the landing and he said: "Contact light!" Armstrong was supposed to immed=
iately shut the engine down, as the engineers suspected the pressure caused=
 by the engine's own exhaust reflecting off the lunar surface could make it=
 explode, but he forgot. Three seconds later, <i>Eagle</i> landed and Armst=
rong shut the engine down.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChaikin1994199_128-0"=
 class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cit=
e_note-FOOTNOTEChaikin1994199-128"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>127=
<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Aldrin immediately said "Ok=
ay, engine stop. ACA=E2=80=94out of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Detent" title=3D"Detent">detent</a>." Armstrong acknowledged: "Out of det=
ent. Auto." Aldrin continued: "Mode control=E2=80=94both auto. Descent engi=
ne command override off. Engine arm=E2=80=94off. 413 is in."<sup id=3D"cite=
_ref-FOOTNOTEMindell2008226_129-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMindell2008226-129"><span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>128<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a>=
</sup>
</p>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Apollo_11_Landing_Site_%26_West_Crater.png" c=
lass=3D"mw-file-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikip=
edia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Apollo_11_Landing_Site_%26_West_Crater.png/250px-Ap=
ollo_11_Landing_Site_%26_West_Crater.png" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"250" =
height=3D"250" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"1400" data-file=
-height=3D"1400"></a><figcaption>The landing site relative to <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_(lunar_crater)" title=3D"West (lunar crate=
r)">West crater</a></figcaption></figure>
<p>ACA was the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spacecraft_attitude=
_control" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Spacecraft attitude control">Attit=
ude Control Assembly</a>=E2=80=94the LM's control stick. Output went to the=
 LGC to command the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reaction_contr=
ol_system" title=3D"Reaction control system">reaction control system</a> (R=
CS) jets to fire. "Out of Detent" meant the stick had moved away from its c=
entered position; it was spring-centered like the turn indicator in a car. =
Address 413 of the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_Abort_Gu=
idance_System" title=3D"Apollo Abort Guidance System">Abort Guidance System=
</a> (AGS) contained the variable that indicated the LM had landed.<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-ALSJ_1_10-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-ALSJ_1-10"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</=
span>10<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p><i>Eagle</i> landed at 20:17:40 UTC on Sunday July 20 with 216 pound=
s (98&nbsp;kg) of usable fuel remaining. Information available to the crew =
and mission controllers during the landing showed the LM had enough fuel fo=
r another 25 seconds of powered flight before an abort without touchdown wo=
uld have become unsafe,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-ALSJ_1_10-2" class=3D"reference"=
><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-ALSJ_1-10"><s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>10<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span><=
/a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000295_130-0" class=3D"referenc=
e"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrl=
off2000295-130"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>129<span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">]</span></a></sup> but post-mission analysis showed that the real=
 figure was probably closer to 50 seconds.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Horizons2013_=
131-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_=
11#cite_note-Horizons2013-131"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>130<spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Apollo 11 landed with less fuel=
 than most subsequent missions, and the astronauts encountered a premature =
low fuel warning. This was later found to be the result of the propellant <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slosh_dynamics" title=3D"Slosh dyna=
mics">sloshing</a> more than expected, uncovering a fuel sensor. On subsequ=
ent missions, extra <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slosh_baffle" =
title=3D"Slosh baffle">anti-slosh baffles</a> were added to the tanks to pr=
event this.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-ALSJ_1_10-3" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-ALSJ_1-10"><span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">[</span>10<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Armstrong acknowledged Aldrin's completion of the post-landing check=
list with "Engine arm is off", before responding to the CAPCOM, Charles Duk=
e, with the words, "Houston, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tranq=
uility_Base" title=3D"Tranquility Base">Tranquility Base</a> here. The <i><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_Module_Eagle" title=3D"Lunar =
Module Eagle">Eagle</a></i> has landed." Armstrong's unrehearsed change of =
call sign from "Eagle" to "Tranquility Base" emphasized to listeners that l=
anding was complete and successful.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-132" class=3D"refere=
nce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-132"><spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>131<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></=
a></sup><sup class=3D"noprint Inline-Template noprint noexcerpt Template-Fa=
ct" style=3D"white-space:nowrap;">[<i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Wikipedia:Verifiability#Questionable_sources" title=3D"Wikipedia:Verifi=
ability"><span title=3D"The History Channel was a questionable source in 20=
03, as evidenced by the controversy surrounding The Men Who Killed Kennedy.=
 (November 2025)">better&nbsp;source&nbsp;needed</span></a></i>]</sup> Duke=
 expressed the relief at Mission Control: "Roger, Twan=E2=80=94Tranquility,=
 we copy you on the ground. You got a bunch of guys about to turn blue. We'=
re breathing again. Thanks a lot."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-ALSJ_1_10-4" class=3D=
"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-AL=
SJ_1-10"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>10<span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-133" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-133"><span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">[</span>132<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><span><span clas=
