iter</a> (LRO) of the Apollo 11 landing site</figcaption></figure>
<p>Two and a half hours after landing, before preparations began for the EV=
A, Aldrin radioed to Earth:
</p>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1244412712"><blockquote class=3D"templatequote"><p>This is the LM pilot. I'=
d like to take this opportunity to ask every person listening in, whoever a=
nd wherever they may be, to pause for a moment and contemplate the events o=
f the past few hours and to give thanks in his or her own way.<sup id=3D"ci=
te_ref-134" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ap=
ollo_11#cite_note-134"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>133<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></p></blockquote>
<p>He then took <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eucharist" title=
=3D"Eucharist">communion</a> privately. At this time NASA was still fightin=
g a lawsuit brought by atheist <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mad=
alyn_Murray_O%27Hair" title=3D"Madalyn Murray O'Hair">Madalyn Murray O'Hair=
</a> (who had objected to the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apol=
lo_8_Genesis_reading" title=3D"Apollo 8 Genesis reading">Apollo&nbsp;8 crew=
 reading from the Book of Genesis</a>) demanding that their astronauts refr=
ain from broadcasting religious activities while in space. For this reason,=
 Aldrin chose to refrain from directly mentioning taking communion on the M=
oon. Aldrin was an elder at the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/We=
bster,_Texas" title=3D"Webster, Texas">Webster</a> <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Presbyterianism" title=3D"Presbyterianism">Presbyterian Ch=
urch</a>, and his communion kit was prepared by the pastor of the church, D=
ean Woodruff. Webster Presbyterian possesses the chalice used on the Moon a=
nd commemorates the event each year on the Sunday closest to July 20.<sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChaikin1994204,_623_135-0" class=3D"reference"><a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChaikin19942=
04,_623-135"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>134<span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">]</span></a></sup> The schedule for the mission called for the astro=
nauts to follow the landing with a five-hour sleep period, but they chose t=
o begin preparations for the EVA early, thinking they would be unable to sl=
eep.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMission_Evaluation_Team196921=E2=80=9322_13=
6-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11=
#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMission_Evaluation_Team196921%E2%80%9322-136"><span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>135<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></su=
p>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Lunar_surface_operations">L=
unar surface operations</h3></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1353705441"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main article: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tranquility_Base" ti=
tle=3D"Tranquility Base">Tranquility Base</a></div>
<p>Preparations for <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neil_Armstrong=
" title=3D"Neil Armstrong">Neil Armstrong</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Buzz_Aldrin" title=3D"Buzz Aldrin">Buzz Aldrin</a> to walk =
on the Moon began at 23:43 UTC.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000107_13=
-2" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#=
cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000107-13"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>13=
<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> These took longer than expe=
cted; three and a half hours instead of two.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-ALSJ_3_137-=
0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#c=
ite_note-ALSJ_3-137"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>136<span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> During training on Earth, everything requ=
ired had been neatly laid out in advance, but on the Moon the cabin contain=
ed a large number of other items as well, such as checklists, food packets,=
 and tools.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMission_Evaluation_Team196922_138-0"=
 class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cit=
e_note-FOOTNOTEMission_Evaluation_Team196922-138"><span class=3D"cite-brack=
et">[</span>137<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Six hours an=
d thirty-nine minutes after landing, Armstrong and Aldrin were ready to go =
outside, and <i>Eagle</i> was depressurized.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECor=
tright1975215_139-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECortright1975215-139"><span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">[</span>138<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p><i>Eagle</i><span class=3D"nowrap" style=3D"padding-left:0.1em;">'</=
span>s hatch was opened at 02:39:33.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEOrloff20001=
07_13-3" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apoll=
o_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000107-13"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>13<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Armstrong initially ha=
d some difficulties squeezing through the hatch with his <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primary_life_support_system" title=3D"Primary life s=
upport system">portable life support system</a> (PLSS).<sup id=3D"cite_ref-=
ALSJ_3_137-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Apollo_11#cite_note-ALSJ_3-137"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>136<sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Some of the highest heart rate=
s recorded from Apollo astronauts occurred during LM egress and ingress.<su=
p id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEJohnstonDietleinBerry1975115=E2=80=93120_140-0" cl=
ass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_n=
ote-FOOTNOTEJohnstonDietleinBerry1975115%E2%80%93120-140"><span class=3D"ci=
te-bracket">[</span>139<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> At 0=
2:51 Armstrong began his descent to the lunar surface. The remote-control u=
nit on his chest kept him from seeing his feet. Climbing down the nine-rung=
 ladder, Armstrong pulled a D-ring to deploy the modular equipment stowage =
assembly (MESA) folded against <i>Eagle</i><span class=3D"nowrap" style=3D"=
padding-left:0.1em;">'s</span> side and activate the TV camera.<sup id=3D"c=
ite_ref-141" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A=
pollo_11#cite_note-141"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>140<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-ALSJ_4_142-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note=
-ALSJ_4-142"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>141<span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Apollo 11 used <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slow-scan_te=
levision" title=3D"Slow-scan television">slow-scan television</a> (TV) inco=
mpatible with broadcast TV, so it was displayed on a special monitor and a =
conventional TV camera viewed this monitor (thus, a broadcast of a broadcas=
t), significantly reducing the quality of the picture.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-B=
lunder_5_143-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Apollo_11#cite_note-Blunder_5-143"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>1=
42<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The signal was received a=
t <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldstone_Deep_Space_Communicati=
ons_Complex" title=3D"Goldstone Deep Space Communications Complex">Goldston=
e</a> in the United States, but with better fidelity by <a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honeysuckle_Creek_Tracking_Station" title=3D"Honeysuc=
kle Creek Tracking Station">Honeysuckle Creek Tracking Station</a> near <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canberra" title=3D"Canberra">Canberra=
</a> in Australia. Minutes later the feed was switched to the more sensitiv=
e <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parkes_Observatory" title=3D"Par=
kes Observatory">Parkes radio telescope</a> in Australia.<sup id=3D"cite_re=
f-FOOTNOTESarkissian2001287_144-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTESarkissian2001287-144"><sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>143<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span><=
/a></sup> Despite some technical and weather difficulties, black and white =
images of the first lunar EVA were received and broadcast to at least 600&n=
bsp;million people on Earth.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTESarkissian2001287_1=
44-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_1=
1#cite_note-FOOTNOTESarkissian2001287-144"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</=
span>143<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Copies of this vide=
o in broadcast format were saved and are widely available, but <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11_missing_tapes" title=3D"Apollo 11 mi=
ssing tapes">recordings of the original slow scan source transmission from =
the lunar surface</a> were likely destroyed during routine magnetic tape re=
-use at NASA.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Blunder_5_143-1" class=3D"reference"><a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-Blunder_5-143"><spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>142<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></=
a></sup>
</p>
