<p>After describing the surface dust as "very fine-grained" and "almost lik=
e a powder",<sup id=3D"cite_ref-ALSJ_4_142-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-ALSJ_4-142"><span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>141<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></s=
up> at 02:56:15,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-AT-20190723_145-0" class=3D"reference">=
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-AT-20190723-14=
5"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>144<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</=
span></a></sup> six and a half hours after landing, Armstrong stepped off <=
i>Eagle</i><span class=3D"nowrap" style=3D"padding-left:0.1em;">'s</span> l=
anding pad and declared: "That's one small step for [a] man, one giant leap=
 for mankind."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-transcript_147-0" class=3D"reference"><a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-transcript-147"><=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>b<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span><=
/a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000108_148-0" class=3D"referenc=
e"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrl=
off2000108-148"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>146<span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-149" class=3D"reference"><a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-149"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>147<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
>
</p><p>Armstrong intended to say "That's one small step for a man", but the=
 word "a" is not audible in the transmission, and thus was not initially re=
ported by most observers of the live broadcast. When later asked about his =
quote, Armstrong said he believed he said "for a man", and subsequent print=
ed versions of the quote included the "a" in square brackets. One explanati=
on for the absence may be that his accent caused him to slur the words "for=
 a" together; another is the intermittent nature of the audio and video lin=
ks to Earth, partly because of storms near Parkes Observatory. A more recen=
t digital analysis of the tape claims to reveal the "a" may have been spoke=
n but obscured by static. Other analysis points to the claims of static and=
 slurring as "face-saving fabrication", and that Armstrong himself later ad=
mitted to misspeaking the line.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-150" class=3D"reference"=
><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-150"><span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>148<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></=
sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-151" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-151"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</spa=
n>149<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-152=
" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#ci=
te_note-152"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>150<span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>About seven minutes after stepping onto the Moon's surface, Armstron=
g collected a contingency soil sample using a sample bag on a stick. He the=
n folded the bag and tucked it into a pocket on his right thigh. This was t=
o guarantee there would be some lunar soil brought back in case an emergenc=
y required the astronauts to abandon the EVA and return to the LM.<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-153" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Apollo_11#cite_note-153"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>151<span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Twelve minutes after the sample w=
as collected,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000108_148-1" class=3D"refe=
rence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOT=
EOrloff2000108-148"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>146<span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> he removed the TV camera from the MESA and=
 made a panoramic sweep, then mounted it on a tripod.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-AL=
SJ_3_137-2" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ap=
ollo_11#cite_note-ALSJ_3-137"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>136<span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The TV camera cable remained par=
tly coiled and presented a tripping hazard throughout the EVA. Still photog=
raphy was accomplished with a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hass=
elblad" title=3D"Hasselblad">Hasselblad</a> camera that could be operated h=
and-held or mounted on Armstrong's <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Apollo/Skylab_A7L" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Apollo/Skylab A7L">Apoll=
o space suit</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMission_Evaluation_Team196923_=
154-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_=
11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMission_Evaluation_Team196923-154"><span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">[</span>152<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Aldrin=
 joined Armstrong on the surface. He described the view with the simple phr=
ase: "Magnificent desolation."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-ALSJ_4_142-2" class=3D"re=
ference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-ALSJ_=
4-142"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>141<span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Armstrong said moving in the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Gravitation_of_the_Moon" title=3D"Gravitation of the Moon">lunar gravity<=
/a>, one-sixth of Earth's, was "even perhaps easier than the simulations&nb=
sp;... It's absolutely no trouble to walk around."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-ALSJ_=
4_142-3" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apoll=
o_11#cite_note-ALSJ_4-142"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>141<span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Aldrin joined him on the surface an=
d tested methods for moving around, including two-footed kangaroo hops. The=
 PLSS backpack created a tendency to tip backward, but neither astronaut ha=
d serious problems maintaining balance. Loping became the preferred method =
of movement. The astronauts reported that they needed to plan their movemen=
ts six or seven steps ahead. The fine soil was quite slippery. Aldrin remar=
ked that moving from sunlight into <i>Eagle</i><span class=3D"nowrap" style=
=3D"padding-left:0.1em;">'s</span> shadow produced no temperature change in=
side the suit, but the helmet was warmer in sunlight, so he felt cooler in =
shadow.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-ALSJ_4_142-4" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-ALSJ_4-142"><span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">[</span>141<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The=
 MESA failed to provide a stable work platform and was in shadow, slowing w=
ork somewhat. As they worked, the moonwalkers kicked up gray dust, which so=
iled the outer part of their suits.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMission_Eval=
uation_Team196923_154-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMission_Evaluation_Team196923-154"><=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>152<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span=
></a></sup>
</p>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Buzz_salutes_the_U.S._Flag.jpg" class=3D"mw-f=
ile-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons=
/thumb/d/dd/Buzz_salutes_the_U.S._Flag.jpg/250px-Buzz_salutes_the_U.S._Flag=
.jpg" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"250" height=3D"252" class=3D"mw-file-elem=
ent" data-file-width=3D"3929" data-file-height=3D"3967"></a><figcaption>Ald=
rin salutes the deployed United States flag on the <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_surface" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Lunar surfac=
e">lunar surface</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The astronauts planted the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luna=
r_Flag_Assembly" title=3D"Lunar Flag Assembly">Lunar Flag Assembly</a> cont=
aining a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_of_the_United_States=
" title=3D"Flag of the United States">flag of the United States</a> on the =
lunar surface, in clear view of the TV camera. Aldrin remembered, "Of all t=
he jobs I had to do on the Moon the one I wanted to go the smoothest was th=
e flag raising."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-theattic_155-0" class=3D"reference"><a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-theattic-155"><sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>153<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span><=
/a></sup> But the astronauts struggled with the telescoping rod and could o=
nly insert the pole about 2 inches (5&nbsp;cm) into the hard lunar surface.=
 Aldrin was afraid it might topple in front of TV viewers, but gave "a cris=
p West Point salute".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-theattic_155-1" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-theattic-155=
"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>153<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</s=
pan></a></sup>
</p><p>Before Aldrin could take a photo of Armstrong with the flag, Preside=
nt Richard Nixon spoke to them through a telephone-radio transmission, whic=
h Nixon called "the most historic phone call ever made from the White House=
."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-156" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-156"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>=
154<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Nixon originally had a l=
ong speech prepared to read during the phone call, but Frank Borman, who wa=
s at the White House as a NASA liaison during Apollo 11, convinced Nixon to=
 keep his words brief.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBormanSerling1988237=E2=
=80=93238_157-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBormanSerling1988237%E2%80%93238-157"><span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>155<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a>=
</sup>
</p>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1244412712"><blockquote class=3D"templatequote"><p><b>Nixon:</b> Hello, Nei=
l and Buzz. I'm talking to you by telephone from the Oval Room at the White=
 House. And this certainly has to be the most historic telephone call ever =
made from the White House. I just can't tell you how proud we all are of wh=
at you have done. For every American, this has to be the proudest day of ou=
r lives. And for people all over the world, I am sure that they too join wi=
th Americans in recognizing what an immense feat this is. Because of what y=
ou have done, the heavens have become a part of man's world. And as you tal=
k to us from the Sea of Tranquility, it inspires us to redouble our efforts=
 to bring peace and tranquility to Earth. For one priceless moment in the w=
hole history of man, all the people on this Earth are truly one: one in the=
ir pride in what you have done, and one in our prayers that you will return=
 safely to Earth.</p><div class=3D"paragraphbreak" style=3D"margin-top:0.5e=
m"></div>
<p><b>Armstrong:</b> Thank you, Mr. President. It's a great honor and privi=
lege for us to be here, representing not only the United States, but men of=
 peace of all nations, and with interest and a curiosity, and men with a vi=
sion for the future. It's an honor for us to be able to participate here to=
day.
</p>
<p><b>Nixon:</b> Thank you very much, and I look forward, all of us look fo=
rward, to seeing you on the Hornet on Thursday.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-158" cla=
ss=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_no=
te-158"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>156<span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-159" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-159"><span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">[</span>157<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></p></b=
lockquote>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Buzz_Aldrin%27s_bootprint_on_the_Moon,_AS11-4=
0-5877.jpg" class=3D"mw-file-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikime=
dia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Buzz_Aldrin%27s_bootprint_on_the_Moon%=
2C_AS11-40-5877.jpg/250px-Buzz_Aldrin%27s_bootprint_on_the_Moon%2C_AS11-40-=
5877.jpg" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"250" height=3D"250" class=3D"mw-file-=
element" data-file-width=3D"10556" data-file-height=3D"10569"></a><figcapti=
on>Aldrin's bootprint, part of an experiment to test the properties of the =
lunar <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regolith" title=3D"Regolith"=
>regolith</a></figcaption></figure>
<p>They deployed the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Early_Apollo_=
Scientific_Experiments_Package" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Early Apollo=
 Scientific Experiments Package">EASEP</a>, which included a <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Passive_Seismic_Experiment_Package" title=3D"Pas=
sive Seismic Experiment Package">Passive Seismic Experiment Package</a> use=
d to measure <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moonquake" class=3D"m=
w-redirect" title=3D"Moonquake">moonquakes</a> and a <a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Laser_Ranging_Retroflector" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=
=3D"Laser Ranging Retroflector">retroreflector</a> array used for the <a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_laser_ranging_experiment" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Lunar laser ranging experiment">lunar laser rangi=
ng experiment</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-EASEP_Deployment_and_Closeout_160-0" =
class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite=
_note-EASEP_Deployment_and_Closeout-160"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>158<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Then Armstrong walked=
 196 feet (60&nbsp;m) from the LM to take photographs at the rim of <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_West_(lunar_crater)" title=3D"Litt=
le West (lunar crater)">Little West Crater</a> while Aldrin collected two <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Core_sample" title=3D"Core sample">=
core samples</a>. He used the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geol=
ogist%27s_hammer" title=3D"Geologist's hammer">geologist's hammer</a> to po=
und in the tubes=E2=80=94the only time the hammer was used on Apollo 11=E2=
=80=94but was unable to penetrate more than 6 inches (15&nbsp;cm) deep. The=
 astronauts then collected rock samples using scoops and tongs on extension=
 handles.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHarland199928=E2=80=9329_161-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note=
-FOOTNOTEHarland199928%E2%80%9329-161"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span=
>159<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Many of the surface activities took longer than expected, so they ha=
d to stop documenting sample collection halfway through the allotted 34 min=
utes. Aldrin shoveled 6 kilograms (13&nbsp;lb) of soil into the box of rock=
s to pack them in tightly.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHarland199928=E2=80=
=9329_161-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A=
pollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHarland199928%E2%80%9329-161"><span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">[</span>159<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Two t=
ypes of rocks were found in the geological samples: <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Basalt" title=3D"Basalt">basalt</a> and <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breccia" title=3D"Breccia">breccia</a>.<sup id=3D"c=
ite_ref-162" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A=
pollo_11#cite_note-162"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>160<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Three new minerals were discovered in =
the rock samples collected by the astronauts: <a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Armalcolite" title=3D"Armalcolite">armalcolite</a>, <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tranquillityite" title=3D"Tranquillityite">tr=
anquillityite</a>, and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyroxferroi=
te" title=3D"Pyroxferroite">pyroxferroite</a>. Armalcolite was named after =
Armstrong, Aldrin, and Collins. All have subsequently been found on Earth.<=
sup id=3D"cite_ref-163" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-163"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>161=
<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb">=
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Apollo_11_plaque_closeup_on_M=
oon.jpg" class=3D"mw-file-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia=
.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Apollo_11_plaque_closeup_on_Moon.jpg/250p=
x-Apollo_11_plaque_closeup_on_Moon.jpg" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"250" he=
ight=3D"235" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"873" data-file-he=
ight=3D"822"></a><figcaption><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar=
_plaque" title=3D"Lunar plaque">The plaque</a> left on the ladder of <i>Eag=
le</i></figcaption></figure>
<p>While on the surface, Armstrong uncovered a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Lunar_plaque" title=3D"Lunar plaque">plaque</a> mounted on the=
 LM ladder, bearing two drawings of Earth (of the Western and Eastern Hemis=
pheres), an inscription, and signatures of the astronauts and President Nix=
on. The inscription read:
</p>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1244412712"><blockquote class=3D"templatequote"><p>Here men from the planet=
 Earth first set foot upon the Moon July 1969, A. D. We came in peace for a=
ll mankind.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-ALSJ_4_142-5" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-ALSJ_4-142"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>141<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
></p></blockquote>
<p>At the behest of the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nixon_admi=
nistration" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Nixon administration">Nixon admi=
nistration</a> to add a reference to God, NASA included the vague date as a=
 reason to include A.D., which stands for <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Anno_Domini" title=3D"Anno Domini">Anno Domini</a> ("in the year of=
 our Lord").<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGardner2017143_164-0" class=3D"refe=
rence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOT=
EGardner2017143-164"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>162<span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Mission Control used a coded phrase to warn Armstrong his metabolic =
rates were high, and that he should slow down. He was moving rapidly from t=
ask to task as time ran out. As metabolic rates remained generally lower th=
an expected for both astronauts throughout the walk, Mission Control grante=
d the astronauts a 15-minute extension.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-EASEP_Deployment=
_and_Closeout_160-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-EASEP_Deployment_and_Closeout-160"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>158<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
> In a 2010 interview, Armstrong explained that NASA limited the first moon=
walk's time and distance because there was no empirical proof of how much c=
ooling water the astronauts' PLSS backpacks would consume to handle their b=
ody heat generation while working on the Moon.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-165" clas=
s=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_not=
e-165"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>163<span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Lunar_ascent">Lunar ascent<=
/h3></div>
<p>Aldrin entered <i>Eagle</i> first. With some difficulty the astronauts l=
ifted film and two sample boxes containing 21.55 kilograms (47.5&nbsp;lb) o=
f lunar surface material to the LM hatch using a flat cable pulley device c=
alled the Lunar Equipment Conveyor (LEC). This proved to be an inefficient =
tool, and later missions preferred to carry equipment and samples up to the=
 LM by hand.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-ALSJ_3_137-3" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-ALSJ_3-137"><span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>136<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></s=
up> Armstrong reminded Aldrin of a bag of memorial items in his sleeve pock=
et, and Aldrin tossed the bag down. Armstrong then jumped onto the ladder's=
 third rung, and climbed into the LM. After transferring to LM <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Life_support" title=3D"Life support">life supp=
ort</a>, the explorers lightened the ascent stage for the return to lunar o=
rbit by tossing out their PLSS backpacks, lunar overshoes, an empty Hasselb=
lad camera, and other equipment.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-166" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-166"><span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>164<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a><=
/sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-167" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-167"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>165<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The hatch was closed =
again at 05:11:13. They then pressurized the LM and settled down to sleep.<=
sup id=3D"cite_ref-ALSJ_6_168-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-ALSJ_6-168"><span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">[</span>166<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Aldrin_Looks_Back_at_Tranquili=
ty_Base_-_GPN-2000-001102.jpg" class=3D"mw-file-description"><img src=3D"ht=
tps://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Aldrin_Looks_Back_a=
t_Tranquility_Base_-_GPN-2000-001102.jpg/500px-Aldrin_Looks_Back_at_Tranqui=
lity_Base_-_GPN-2000-001102.jpg" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"350" height=3D=
"350" style=3D"--mw-file-upright: 1.4" class=3D"mw-file-element mw-file-upr=
ight" data-file-width=3D"3904" data-file-height=3D"3904"></a><figcaption>Al=
drin next to the Passive Seismic Experiment Package with the <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_Lunar_Module" title=3D"Apollo Lunar Modul=
e">Lunar Module</a> <i>Eagle</i> in the background</figcaption></figure>
<p>Presidential speech writer <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Will=
iam_Safire" title=3D"William Safire">William Safire</a> had prepared an <i>=
In Event of Moon Disaster</i> announcement for Nixon to read in the event t=
he Apollo 11 astronauts were stranded on the Moon.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-169" =
class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite=
_note-169"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>167<span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">]</span></a></sup> The remarks were in a memo from Safire to Nixon's <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House_Chief_of_Staff" title=
=3D"White House Chief of Staff">White House Chief of Staff</a> <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H._R._Haldeman" title=3D"H. R. Haldeman">H. R.=
 Haldeman</a>, in which Safire suggested a protocol the administration migh=
t follow in reaction to such a disaster.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-170" class=3D"r=
eference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-170"=
><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>168<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</sp=
an></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-safire_171-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-safire-171"><span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>169<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></s=
up> According to the plan, Mission Control would "close down communications=
" with the LM, and a clergyman would "commend their souls to the deepest of=
 the deep" in a public ritual likened to <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Burial_at_sea" title=3D"Burial at sea">burial at sea</a>. The last l=
ine of the prepared text contained an allusion to <a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Rupert_Brooke" title=3D"Rupert Brooke">Rupert Brooke</a>'s =
World War I poem "<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Soldier_(poe=
m)" title=3D"The Soldier (poem)">The Soldier</a>".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-safir=
e_171-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apoll=
o_11#cite_note-safire-171"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>169<span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The script for the speech does not =
make reference to Collins; as he remained onboard <i><a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Command_module_Columbia" title=3D"Command module Columbi=
a">Columbia</a></i> in orbit around the Moon, it was expected that he would=
 be able to return the module to Earth in the event of a mission failure.<s=
up id=3D"cite_ref-172" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-172"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>170<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>While moving inside the cabin, Aldrin accidentally damaged the <a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circuit_breaker" title=3D"Circuit break=
er">circuit breaker</a> that would arm the main engine for liftoff from the=
 Moon. There was a concern this would prevent firing the engine, stranding =
them on the Moon. The nonconductive tip of a Duro <a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Marker_pen" title=3D"Marker pen">felt-tip pen</a><sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-173" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Apollo_11#cite_note-173"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>171<span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> was sufficient to activate the switc=
h.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-ALSJ_6_168-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-ALSJ_6-168"><span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">[</span>166<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>After more than <span class=3D"frac">21<span class=3D"sr-only">+</sp=
an><span class=3D"num">1</span>=E2=81=84<span class=3D"den">2</span></span>=
 hours on the lunar surface, in addition to the scientific instruments, the=
 astronauts left behind: an <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo=
_1" title=3D"Apollo 1">Apollo 1</a> mission patch in memory of astronauts&n=
bsp;<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roger_B._Chaffee" title=3D"Rog=
er B. Chaffee">Roger Chaffee</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Gus_Grissom" title=3D"Gus Grissom">Gus Grissom</a>, and <a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ed_White_(astronaut)" title=3D"Ed White (astronaut)">=
Edward White</a>, who died when their command module caught fire during a t=
est in January 1967; two memorial medals of Soviet cosmonauts <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir_Komarov" title=3D"Vladimir Komarov">Vl=
adimir Komarov</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuri_Gagari=
n" title=3D"Yuri Gagarin">Yuri Gagarin</a>, who died in 1967 and 1968 respe=
ctively; a memorial bag containing a gold replica of an olive branch as a t=
raditional symbol of peace; and a silicon message disk carrying the <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11_goodwill_messages" title=3D"Apo=
llo 11 goodwill messages">goodwill statements</a> by presidents Eisenhower,=
 Kennedy, Johnson, and Nixon along with messages from leaders of 73 countri=
es around the world.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-174" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-174"><span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">[</span>172<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The di=
sk also carries a listing of the leadership of the US Congress, a listing o=
f members of the four committees of the House and Senate responsible for th=
e NASA legislation, and the names of NASA's past and then-current top manag=
ement.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-175" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-175"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</s=
pan>173<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Apollo_11_photo_map.svg" class=3D"mw-file-des=
cription"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/=
2/23/Apollo_11_photo_map.svg/330px-Apollo_11_photo_map.svg.png" decoding=3D=
"async" width=3D"330" height=3D"330" style=3D"--mw-file-upright: 1.3" class=
=3D"mw-file-element mw-file-upright" data-file-width=3D"709" data-file-heig=
ht=3D"709"></a><figcaption>A map of the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Tranquility_Base" title=3D"Tranquility Base">Tranquility Base</a> lan=
ding site and photos taken</figcaption></figure>
<p>After about seven hours of rest, the crew was awakened by Houston to pre=
pare for the return flight.
At that time, unknown to them, some hundred kilometers away from them the S=
oviet probe <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luna_15" title=3D"Luna=
 15">Luna 15</a> was about to descend and impact. Despite having been known=
 to be orbiting the Moon at the same time, through a ground-breaking precau=
tious goodwill exchange of data, the mission control of Luna 15 unexpectedl=
y hastened its robotic <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sample-retu=
rn_mission" title=3D"Sample-return mission">sample-return mission</a>, init=
iating descent, in an attempt to return before Apollo 11.<sup id=3D"cite_re=
f-audio_176-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Apollo_11#cite_note-audio-176"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>174<sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Just two hours before Apollo 1=
1's launch, Luna 15 crashed at 15:50 UTC, with British astronomers monitori=
ng Luna 15 and recording the situation one commented: "I say, this has real=
ly been drama of the highest order",<sup id=3D"cite_ref-a357_177-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note=
-a357-177"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>175<span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">]</span></a></sup> bringing the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Space_Race" title=3D"Space Race">Space Race</a> to a culmination.<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-178" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Apollo_11#cite_note-178"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>176<span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Roughly two hours later, at 17:54:00 UTC, the Apollo 11 crew on the =
surface safely lifted off in <i>Eagle</i><span class=3D"nowrap" style=3D"pa=
dding-left:0.1em;">'s</span> ascent stage to rejoin Collins aboard <i>Colum=
bia</i> in lunar orbit.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000108_148-2" cla=
ss=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_no=
te-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000108-148"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>146<span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Film taken from the LM ascent st=
age upon liftoff from the Moon reveals the American flag, planted some 25 f=
eet (8&nbsp;m) from the descent stage, whipping violently in the exhaust of=
 the ascent stage engine. Aldrin looked up in time to witness the flag topp=
le: "The ascent stage of the LM separated&nbsp;... I was concentrating on t=
he computers, and Neil was studying the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Attitude_indicator" title=3D"Attitude indicator">attitude indicator</=
a>, but I looked up long enough to see the flag fall over."<sup id=3D"cite_=
ref-FOOTNOTECollinsAldrin1975219_179-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollinsAldrin1975219-=
179"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>177<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]=
</span></a></sup> Subsequent Apollo missions planted their flags farther fr=
om the LM.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-180" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-180"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>178<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Columbia_in_lunar_orbit"><i=
>Columbia</i> in lunar orbit</h3></div>
<p>During his day flying solo around the Moon, Collins never felt lonely. A=
lthough it has been said "not since <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Adam" title=3D"Adam">Adam</a> has any human known such solitude",<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001402_181-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollins2001402-181=
"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>179<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</s=
pan></a></sup> Collins felt very much a part of the mission. In his autobio=
graphy he wrote: "this venture has been structured for three men, and I con=
sider my third to be as necessary as either of the other two".<sup id=3D"ci=
te_ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001402_181-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollins2001402-181"><spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>179<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></=
a></sup> In the 48 minutes of each orbit when he was out of radio contact w=
ith the Earth while <i>Columbia</i> passed round the far side of the Moon, =
the feeling he reported was not fear or loneliness, but rather "awareness, =
anticipation, satisfaction, confidence, almost exultation".<sup id=3D"cite_=
ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001402_181-2" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollins2001402-181"><span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>179<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a><=
/sup>
</p><p>One of Collins' first tasks was to identify the Lunar Module on the =
ground. To give Collins an idea where to look, Mission Control radioed that=
 they believed the Lunar Module landed about 4 miles (6.4&nbsp;km) off targ=
et. Each time he passed over the suspected lunar landing site, he tried in =
vain to find the module. On his first orbits on the back side of the Moon, =
Collins performed maintenance activities such as dumping excess water produ=
ced by the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuel_cell" title=3D"Fue=
l cell">fuel cells</a> and preparing the cabin for Armstrong and Aldrin to =
return.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001401=E2=80=93407_182-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note=
-FOOTNOTECollins2001401%E2%80%93407-182"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>180<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Just before he reached the dark side on the third orbit, Mission Con=
trol informed Collins there was a problem with the temperature of the coola=
nt. If it became too cold, parts of <i>Columbia</i> might freeze. Mission C=
ontrol advised him to assume manual control and implement Environmental Con=
trol System Malfunction Procedure 17. Instead, Collins flicked the switch o=
n the system from automatic to manual and back to automatic again, and carr=
ied on with normal housekeeping chores, while keeping an eye on the tempera=
ture.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001406=E2=80=93408,_410_183-0" cla=
ss=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_no=
te-FOOTNOTECollins2001406%E2%80%93408,_410-183"><span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">[</span>181<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>When <i>Columbia</i> came back around to the near side of the Moon a=
gain, Collins was able to report that the problem had been resolved. For th=
e next couple of orbits, he described his time on the back side of the Moon=
 as "relaxing". After Aldrin and Armstrong completed their EVA, Collins sle=
pt so he could be rested for the rendezvous. While the flight plan called f=
or <i>Eagle</i> to meet up with <i>Columbia</i>, Collins was prepared for a=
 contingency in which he would fly <i>Columbia</i> down to meet <i>Eagle</i=
>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollins2001406=E2=80=93408,_410_183-1" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note=
-FOOTNOTECollins2001406%E2%80%93408,_410-183"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>181<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Return">Return</h3></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Earth,_Moon_and_Lunar_Module,_AS11-44-6643_c.=
jpg" class=3D"mw-file-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org=
/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Earth%2C_Moon_and_Lunar_Module%2C_AS11-44-664=
3_c.jpg/250px-Earth%2C_Moon_and_Lunar_Module%2C_AS11-44-6643_c.jpg" decodin=
g=3D"async" width=3D"250" height=3D"239" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-fil=
e-width=3D"4161" data-file-height=3D"3980"></a><figcaption><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_Module_Eagle" title=3D"Lunar Module Eagle"><=
i>Eagle</i></a><span class=3D"nowrap" style=3D"padding-left:0.1em;">'s</spa=
n> ascent stage approaching <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comman=
d_Module_Columbia" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Command Module Columbia">=
<i>Columbia</i></a></figcaption></figure>
<p><i>Eagle</i> rendezvoused with <i>Columbia</i> at 21:24 UTC on July 21, =
and the two docked at 21:35. <i>Eagle</i><span class=3D"nowrap" style=3D"pa=
dding-left:0.1em;">'</span>s ascent stage was jettisoned into lunar orbit a=
t 23:41.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEOrloff2000109_9-3" class=3D"reference">=
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTEOrloff=
2000109-9"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>9<span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">]</span></a></sup> Just before the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Apollo_12" title=3D"Apollo 12">Apollo 12</a> flight, it was noted that =
<i>Eagle</i> was still likely to be orbiting the Moon. Later NASA reports m=
entioned that <i>Eagle</i><span class=3D"nowrap" style=3D"padding-left:0.1e=
m;">'s</span> orbit had decayed, resulting in it impacting in an "uncertain=
 location" on the lunar surface.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-184" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-184"><span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>182<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a><=
/sup> In 2021, however, some calculations show that the lander may still be=
 in orbit.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-185" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-185"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>183<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>On July 23, the last night before splashdown, the three astronauts m=
ade a television broadcast in which Collins commented: "All this is possibl=
e only through the blood, sweat, and tears of a number of people&nbsp;... A=
ll you see is the three of us, but beneath the surface are thousands and th=
ousands of others, and to all of those, I would like to say, 'Thank you ver=
y much'."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECollinsAldrin1975222_186-0" class=3D"r=
eference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOT=
NOTECollinsAldrin1975222-186"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>184<span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Aldrin added: "This has been far=
 more than three men on a mission to the Moon; more, still, than the effort=
s of a government and industry team; more, even, than the efforts of one na=
tion. We feel that this stands as a symbol of the insatiable curiosity of a=
ll mankind to explore the unknown&nbsp;..."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEColl=
insAldrin1975222_186-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollinsAldrin1975222-186"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>184<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
>
</p><p>Armstrong concluded:
</p>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1244412712"><blockquote class=3D"templatequote"><p>The responsibility for t=
his flight lies first with history and with the giants of science who have =
preceded this effort; next with the American people, who have, through thei=
r will, indicated their desire; next with four administrations and their Co=
ngresses, for implementing that will; and then, with the agency and industr=
y teams that built our spacecraft, the Saturn, the Columbia, the Eagle, and=
 the little <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extravehicular_Mobilit=
y_Unit" title=3D"Extravehicular Mobility Unit">EMU</a>, the spacesuit and b=
ackpack that was our small spacecraft out on the lunar surface. We would li=
ke to give special thanks to all those Americans who built the spacecraft; =
who did the construction, design, the tests, and put their hearts and all t=
heir abilities into those craft. To those people tonight, we give a special=
 thank you, and to all the other people that are listening and watching ton=
ight, God bless you. Good night from Apollo 11.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTE=
CollinsAldrin1975222_186-2" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECollinsAldrin1975222-186"><span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>184<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a><=
/sup> </p></blockquote>
<p>On the return to Earth, a bearing at the Guam tracking station failed, p=
otentially preventing communication on the last segment of the Earth return=
. A regular repair was not possible in the available time but the station d=
irector, Charles Force, had his ten-year-old son Greg use his small hands t=
o reach into the housing and pack it with grease. Greg was later thanked by=
 Armstrong.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-187" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-187"><span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>[</span>185<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Splashdown_and_quarantine">=
Splashdown and quarantine</h3></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Splashdown_3.jpg" class=3D"mw-file-descriptio=
n"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Sp=
lashdown_3.jpg/250px-Splashdown_3.jpg" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"250" hei=
ght=3D"240" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"1179" data-file-he=
ight=3D"1131"></a><figcaption><i>Columbia</i> floats on the ocean as Navy d=
ivers assist in retrieving the astronauts.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aircraft_carrier" title=3D"=
Aircraft carrier">aircraft carrier</a> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/USS_Hornet_(CV-12)" title=3D"USS Hornet (CV-12)">USS&nbsp;<i>Hornet</i=
></a>, under the command of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Captai=
n_(United_States_O-6)" title=3D"Captain (United States O-6)">Captain</a> <a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carl_J._Seiberlich" title=3D"Carl J.=
 Seiberlich">Carl J. Seiberlich</a>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECarmichael2=
0103_188-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ap=
ollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECarmichael20103-188"><span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>[</span>186<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> was selected as=
 the primary recovery ship (PRS) for Apollo 11 on June 5, replacing her sis=
ter ship, the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landing_platform_hel=
icopter" title=3D"Landing platform helicopter">LPH</a> <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Princeton_(CV-37)" title=3D"USS Princeton (CV-37)"=
>USS&nbsp;<i>Princeton</i></a>, which had recovered Apollo 10 on May 26. <i=
>Hornet</i> was then at her home port of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Long_Beach,_California" title=3D"Long Beach, California">Long Beach,=
 California</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECarmichael201021_189-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note=
-FOOTNOTECarmichael201021-189"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>187<spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> On reaching <a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl_Harbor" title=3D"Pearl Harbor">Pearl Harbor</a>=
 on July 5, <i>Hornet</i> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embarkat=
ion" title=3D"Embarkation">embarked</a> the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Sikorsky_SH-3_Sea_King" title=3D"Sikorsky SH-3 Sea King">Sikorsky=
 SH-3 Sea King</a> helicopters of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
HSC-4" title=3D"HSC-4">HS-4</a>, a unit which specialized in recovery of Ap=
ollo spacecraft, specialized divers of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Underwater_Demolition_Team" title=3D"Underwater Demolition Team">UDT</=
a> Detachment Apollo, a 35-man NASA recovery team, and about 120 media repr=
esentatives. To make room, most of <i>Hornet</i><span class=3D"nowrap" styl=
e=3D"padding-left:0.1em;">'</span>s air wing was left behind in Long Beach.=
 Special recovery equipment was also loaded, including a <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boilerplate_(spaceflight)" title=3D"Boilerplate (spa=
ceflight)">boilerplate</a> command module used for training.<sup id=3D"cite=
_ref-FOOTNOTECarmichael201038=E2=80=9343,_71=E2=80=9372_190-0" class=3D"ref=
erence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNO=
TECarmichael201038%E2%80%9343,_71%E2%80%9372-190"><span class=3D"cite-brack=
et">[</span>188<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>On July 12, with Apollo 11 still on the launch pad, <i>Hornet</i> de=
parted Pearl Harbor for the recovery area in the central Pacific,<sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECarmichael201085_191-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11#cite_note-FOOTNOTECarmichael201085-19=
1"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>189<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</=
span></a></sup> in the vicinity of <span class=3D"geo-inline"><link rel=3D"=
